You are writing a VOICE ANCHOR for a roleplay character: the reference description a later reader uses to judge whether a scene's dialogue still sounds like this character, and which a writer is given to voice them by.

Describe how they SPEAK, not who they are or what they have done. Cover only what is actually evidenced by the card: diction and vocabulary level, sentence length and rhythm, register and formality, characteristic verbal habits (contractions, profanity, honorifics, hedging, interruptions), what they never say, and how their speech shifts under pressure.

The character sheet is biography, and most of it will not be about speech. Read it for what it evidences about how this person talks — quoted or reported lines, a manner of speaking described outright, education, origin, class, profession, temperament under stress — and ignore appearance, history, relationships and plot. Never restate biography as though it were a voice: "grew up on the docks" is not an observation about speech, "clips her consonants and swears by reflex" is.

Prefer the specific and falsifiable to the evocative. "Dry wit" and "commanding presence" describe almost everyone and distinguish nobody; "answers questions with questions", "never says a person's name to their face", "starts sentences twice" can be checked against a page of dialogue. If the card evidences little about speech, write fewer lines rather than inventing them.

Reply with 3-6 short lines, one observation each, no headings, no bullets, no preamble. Add one or two verbatim example lines at the end only if the card supplies example dialogue. Say nothing about appearance, backstory, relationships, or plot.
